import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Lint Rules as Language Data","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Lint Rules as Language Data","type":"adr","date":"2026-09-08T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"accepted"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0097-lint-rules-as-data.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0097-lint-rules-as-data.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0097-lint-rules-as-data.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0097-lint-rules-as-language-data" tabindex="-1">ADR-0097: Lint Rules as Language Data <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0097-lint-rules-as-language-data" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0097: Lint Rules as Language Data”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0044 (&quot;language as data&quot;) established that engines and tooling are generic and data-driven: a new language is a YAML fragment merged into a <code>Language</code>, never an engine recompile. The formatter (<code>plugin-engine-fmt</code>) and typechecker (<code>plugin-engine-typecheck</code> + <code>*-nbe</code>) already follow this — the fmt/typecheck/test split is generic engine + canonical data.</p><p>The linter did not. <code>plugin-ext-lint</code> hardcoded all eight canonical rules in Rust (<code>rule_unused_import</code>, <code>rule_unbound_wire</code>, …), with a bespoke <code>LangInfo</code> classifier and a token-position zipping hack to recover spans the Pratt AST already carries. ADR-0094&#39;s <code>lint</code> pass (<code>find_matches</code> over <code>TransformDef</code>s) proved a lint rule can be <em>data</em>: <code>simplify-paren</code> was already declared in <code>plugin-ext-lint/data/lint_transforms.yaml</code> — but only as a pattern matcher, with the diagnostic and fix still computed in Rust.</p><p>Meanwhile <code>plugin-engine-lint</code> (the generic AST lint engine) and <code>plugin-ext-lint</code> had grown into parallel implementations that never met: the engine&#39;s <code>LintEngine::lint</code> served embedded hosts (<code>host-tooling</code>, <code>plugin-diag-expression</code>) while the ext crate reimplemented its own walk.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Lint rules are first-class <code>Language</code> data.</p><ol><li><p><strong><code>LintRuleDef</code> fragment</strong> (<code>handl-language::fragments::lint_rule</code>), merged into a new <code>Language::lints: FragmentSet&lt;LintRuleDef&gt;</code> (added with <code>serde(default)</code> so pre-ADR-0097 serialized languages still load). Fields: <code>id</code> (fragment identity), <code>name</code> (the reported rule name — several fragments may share a name as alternative arms of one logical rule), <code>severity</code> (<code>&quot;error&quot;|&quot;warning&quot;|&quot;info&quot;</code>), <code>message</code> (a <code>{key}</code> template), <code>check</code>, optional <code>fix</code>, and the usual <code>replaces</code>/<code>requires</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Two check kinds</strong>, mirroring ADR-0094&#39;s declarative/native split:</p><ul><li><code>check: !Match { node_kind, when? }</code> — node-local matching. <code>when</code> is a <code>TransformCondition</code>, evaluated by the <em>same</em> evaluator the rewrite engine uses (<code>plugin-engine-transform-rewrite::condition::matches_condition</code>, now public). The predicate vocabulary gains <code>!ChildPayloadEq { index?, role?, path?, value }</code> — &quot;the child at role/index has payload <code>value</code> at <code>path</code>&quot; — needed to test a child node&#39;s payload (e.g. a constant <code>true</code>/<code>false</code> condition).</li><li><code>check: !Native &quot;name&quot;</code> — the disciplined escape hatch, resolved against the lint engine&#39;s <code>NativeLintRegistry</code>. Natives receive a <code>LintCtx</code> (language, source, parsed statements, shared <code>ModuleAnalysis</code>) and return <code>NativeHit</code>s (template args + absolute span + optional fix). An unregistered native name is a <code>LangError</code> at lint time — a declared rule with no implementation is a configuration bug, not a pass.</li></ul><p><code>fix:</code> on a <code>Match</code> rule is declarative (<code>!Unwrap { role|index }</code> — replace the node&#39;s source text with its child&#39;s); <code>Native</code> rules compute their own fixes.</p></li><li><p><strong>Module-level engine API</strong> in <code>plugin-engine-lint</code>: <code>parse_module</code> (the bootstrap one-statement-per-parse splitting convention, moved out of <code>plugin-ext-lint</code>), <code>LintModule</code>, <code>ModuleAnalysis::build</code> (per-statement bindings / references / arrow params / imports, classified via <code>AstShapeDef</code> roles and <code>pattern_role</code>, spans taken from <code>AstNode::span</code> + statement offset), and <code>LintEngine::lint_module</code> / <code>lint_source</code>. <code>LintDiagnostic</code> gains <code>rule</code> and <code>fix</code>. The single-AST <code>LintEngine::lint</code> built-ins are unchanged.</p></li><li><p><strong>Canonical natives</strong> ship in <code>plugin-engine-lint</code> (<code>canonical_lint_natives()</code>, wired via <code>LintEngine::with_canonical_lint_natives</code> — the analogue of <code>with_desugar_natives</code>): <code>scope.unbound-wire</code>, <code>scope.shadowed-binding</code>, <code>scope.unused-import</code>, <code>naming-convention</code>, <code>source.missing-module-doc</code>. <code>source.fmt</code> is registered by <code>plugin-ext-lint</code> — it needs <code>plugin-ext-fmt</code>, and engine crates do not depend on ext crates.</p></li><li><p><strong>Canonical rules live in <code>handl-kit-data-canonical/data/lints.yaml</code></strong>, merged by <code>merge_fragments</code> (10th fragment file) and <code>language_from_dir</code> (optional, like <code>transforms.yaml</code>). <code>plugin-ext-lint</code> slims to language assembly, engine wiring, <code>plugin-ext-diag</code> conversion, and the fix fixpoint (~70% of the crate deleted); <code>plugin-ext-diag::Diagnostic::rule</code> widened from <code>&amp;&#39;static str</code> to <code>String</code> because rule names are now data.</p><p>The <code>lint</code>-pass <code>TransformDef</code> in <code>plugin-ext-lint/data/lint_transforms.yaml</code> was later <em>removed</em>: <code>host-lsp</code> builds the <code>simplify-paren</code> quick fix directly from the diagnostic&#39;s <code>fix</code> payload (<code>LintFixOut</code> → <code>plugin-ext-diag::Fix</code>), so the transform-engine <code>lint</code>/<code>lsp-rewrite</code> passes had no remaining consumer. The match predicate now lives solely in <code>lints.yaml</code> as the <code>LintRuleDef</code>.</p></li></ol><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Adding or tuning a canonical lint rule is a <code>lints.yaml</code> edit — no recompile of the rule logic, no <code>plugin-ext-lint</code> release. Third-party languages contribute rules through their own fragments.</li><li>Span quality improved for free: <code>unbound-wire</code>, <code>shadowed-binding</code>, and <code>naming-convention</code> now report real <code>AstNode::span</code> positions (the old token-position zip emitted degenerate <code>0:0</code>/<code>1:1</code> spans). Verified by byte-identical diagnostic <em>sets</em> over <code>handl-packages/</code> + <code>examples/</code> (5,189 diagnostics, positions normalized) against the pre-change binary.</li><li><code>!ChildPayloadEq</code> is shared vocabulary: <code>validate_transforms</code> checks its role/index bounds at <code>Language</code> build like every other condition.</li><li>A language can declare <code>!Native &quot;…&quot;</code> rules its host must register; the canonical <code>scope.*</code> set documents the boundary between data and Rust.</li><li><code>.hnl</code>-authored lint natives landed: the same <code>handl:&lt;mod&gt;.&lt;fn&gt;</code> spelling ADR-0094 added for transforms works for <code>!Native</code> lint checks. <code>NativeLintRegistry</code> gained a generic <code>NativeLintResolver</code> fallback seam (<code>add_resolver</code>, consulted on map miss — <code>plugin-engine-lint</code> stays runtime-free); <code>handl_plugin_lib_handl::lint::HandlNativeLint</code> is the adapter mirroring <code>HandlNativeRewrite</code>: it holds <code>HandlModule</code>s, resolves <code>handl:</code> names, and invokes the function <strong>once per module</strong> with a <code>{source, statements: [{text, offset, root}]}</code> record (node records use the transform shape <code>{kind, span, payload, children}</code> but with spans absolutized). The function returns <code>null</code>/<code>[]</code> or a list of hit records <code>{name?, args?, span?, fix?}</code> mapped to <code>NativeHit</code> (fixes included). Module-level rather than per-node was chosen deliberately: <code>!Native</code> carries no <code>node_kind</code> filter, so a per-node contract could express nothing <code>!Match { node_kind, when }</code> cannot — the point of the seam is the module view <code>!Match</code> cannot reach. Like <code>HandlNativeRewrite</code>, no shipped host wires it yet (canonical <code>lints.yaml</code> declares no <code>handl:</code> rules); hosts opt in via <code>reg.add_resolver(HandlNativeLint::new() .with_module(...).resolver())</code>. See <code>plugin-lib-handl/tests/handl_lint.rs</code>.</li><li>Done: the remaining built-in engine checks (<code>empty-block</code>, <code>unreachable-code</code>, <code>shadowed-binding</code>, <code>unused-binding</code>) are now declared as <code>!Native &quot;builtin.*&quot;</code> rules in <code>crates/handl-kit-data-canonical/data/lints.yaml</code> and implemented in the canonical lint native registry.</li></ul>`,10)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};